BIOS and UEFI:
    |--> ensure critical hardware like SATA devices are functional then locates MBR or GUID PARTITION TABLE
BIOS is legacy, UEFI is faster and more secure


firmware: software coded in non-volitile memory, allows for hardware and software to fnction togerther

BIOS MBR (MASTER BOOT RECORD):
    |--> boot loader
            |--> Windows 2003 and older used NTLDR or New Technology Loader
                  Windows 7 Service Pack 1 and newer uses bootmgr or New Technology Loader

UEFI BOOT MANAGER: 
    |--> Windows bootmgfw.efi or Windows Boot Manager

- both BIOS and UEFI both do hardware checks but BIOS finds the MBR and UEFI loads the EFI PARTITION which contains UEFI BOOT MANAGERS  and then both load th OS 

BIOS or UEFI FIND COMMAND:
    |--> findstr /C:"Detected boot environment" "C:\Windows\Panther\Setupact.log"
          Get-Content C:\Windows\Panther\Setupact.log | Select-String "Detected boot environment"

          bcdedit | findstr /i winload    #winload.exe = BIOS, winload.efi = UE


KERNEL INITIALIZATION:          #KERNEL = HEART OF OS
    |--> KERNEL = "Ntoskrnl.exe" 
          |--> Loads Windows Registry
                Loads device drivers
                Starts the system pagefile (C:\pagefile.sys) --> (pagefile: storage space for processes that are not actively bing used and pushed to the background)
                loads "hal.dll" (hardware abstration layer)


STARTING SUBSYSTEMS:
    |--> SESSION MANAGER SUBSYSTEM "smss.exe"
            |--> Loads environmental variables like %APPDATA% and %COMPUTERNAME%
                  Populates the pagefile located in C:\pagefile.sys
                  Starts the kernel and user mode sub systems
                  Starts a csrss.exe to manage processes and threads for each User Subsystem


KERNEL SUBSYSTEMS:            #NO USER AT ANY LEVEL CAN INTERACT WITH KERNEL MODE PROCESS
    |--> creates an manages every resource available by interacting with drivers, controls
          |--> System power state
                Process creation and threads
                Graphical rendering
                Access Control Lists via the Security Reference Monitor


USERS SUBSYSTEM:
    |--> RUNS A SESSION 0 AND SESSION 1
          |--> SESSION 0 IS THE SYSTEM
                SESSION 1 IS THE USERS

(sutdown or reboot stoppage):
shutdown /r /t 0        # restart, restart time to 0
